{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "professional_principle": "Atomicity groups the enrollment write and seat-count update as one all-or-nothing unit. If either fails, rollback undoes all changes in that transaction. Atomicity is distinct from isolation and capacity-race protection.", "valid_solutions": ["A transaction encompassing both changes, committing together on success and rolling back both on any failure.", "An equivalent conceptual all-or-nothing operation with explicit failure reversal."], "scope": "Credit explicit transaction grouping plus rollback without requiring the word atomicity or an isolation-level name. Evaluate cache safety and concurrent capacity reservation in their own criteria; neither substitutes for nor negates an otherwise correct description of rollback."}, "submission": "Author: SyntheticAuthorCedar\nI use one database transaction to create the enrollment and consume a seat, committing both together. If either fails, rollback undoes both changes. A UNIQUE constraint on (student, course) prevents the same student enrolling twice, but does not reserve capacity for different students. For capacity I perform a conditional update that reduces remaining seats only when the count is positive. I accept the enrollment only if that update succeeds and roll back the transaction if enrollment creation fails. Thus competing requests cannot both consume the same final seat. Cached availability speeds up display reads but may be stale, so cached results never authorize enrollment; the conditional database reservation is authoritative."}
